# =============================================================================
# DeepSeek-V4-Flash MXFP4 at 262144 -- experts stream to the GPU, not the CPU
# =============================================================================
# The 262144 sibling of deepseek-v4-flash-gpu-experts-128k, converted on
# 2026-08-19 (RESULTS §27.3). Same principle: the experts that do not fit in VRAM
# stay in host RAM but are COMPUTED ON THE GPU, which is what happens as soon as
# -rtr is not passed (§21).
#
#   depth   deepseek-v4-flash-256k-kvram (-rtr)   this profile      factor
#   4k          451.2 pp / 20.29 tg                1 100.6 / 18.12   2.44x
#   32k         468.2    / 19.32                   1 636.9 / 17.43   3.50x
#   128k        429.3    / 17.11                   1 258.8 / 15.67   2.93x
#
# Generation costs ~10 %, about twice what the 131072 profile gives up, and the
# reason is arithmetic rather than the strategy: --n-cpu-moe 21 against the old
# profile's 18 is three more expert layers in host RAM at -2.8 % each (§22.2).
# Those layers are what -ub 8192 costs at this context.
#
# The trade is still lopsided in absolute terms. On a 128k prompt:
#   prefill 298 s -> 102 s   saves 196 s
#   a 500-token answer 29.2 s -> 31.9 s   costs 2.7 s
# Three minutes of waiting against under three seconds of slower typing, on a
# profile that exists precisely for long contexts.
#
#   ./serve.sh deepseek-v4-flash-gpu-experts-256k
#   ./serve-deepseek-v4-flash-mxfp4-gpu-experts-256k.sh
#
# The -rtr version is still available as deepseek-v4-flash-256k-kvram, and is the
# fallback if this one misbehaves -- it is a different code path, which is the
# point of keeping it.
# =============================================================================

# The 262144 sibling of `deepseek-v4-flash-128k-kvram`: same three levers
# (-rtr, -nkvo + manual split, big -ub), retuned for the bigger KV.
#
#   MEASURED at 130k depth (RESULTS §12.1), caches on, vs the stock §9.2 config:
#     stock (--fit 8192, caches on)   232.6 pp / 12.36 tg
#     this profile                    402.5 pp / 13.38 tg     +73 % / +8 %
#   The gain is *bigger* than at 128k because --fit is worse here: at margin
#   8192 it leaves only 73.0 GiB of weights on the GPU; the manual split gets
#   89.4 GiB up there, i.e. 16.3 GiB of experts out of DDR5.
#
#   ./serve.sh deepseek-v4-flash-256k-kvram
#   ./serve-deepseek-v4-flash-mxfp4-kvram-256k.sh
#
# Differences from the 128k sibling, all forced by the larger context:
#   * --n-cpu-moe 18, not 17 -- the compute buffer grows with context (2.8 GiB
#     here vs 1.8 at 128k, both ub 2048) and n17 CUDA-OOMs (VERIFIED).
#   * checkpoints cost more here: keeping them costs 24 % generation (17.55 ->
#     13.38), not the ~2 % of 128k -- their overhead scales with the 11 GiB KV.
#     Still ON, because a re-send at 130k depth would otherwise re-prefill for
#     ~5 minutes. For single-shot batch work over huge prompts, override with:
#       IK_EXTRA_ARGS="-mla 3 -fidx --reasoning-format deepseek -nkvo -ctx-ckpt 0 --cache-ram 0"
#
# Same robustness caveats as the 128k sibling: manual placement (~3 GiB VRAM
# free), and -rtr forces --no-mmap so every start re-reads the model.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

: "${IK_MODEL_GLOB:=${IK_MODELS_ROOT}/lmstudio-community/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-GGUF/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-MXFP4-00001-of-*.gguf}"

: "${IK_ALIAS:=deepseek-v4-flash-gpu-experts-256k}"
: "${IK_CTX:=262144}"

# Manual split -- --fit would leave 16 GiB of VRAM to rot (see header). Margin
# 4096 does not even load at this context (7.1 GiB compute buffer under --fit).
: "${IK_FIT:=0}"
# 21, and it is the floor rather than a preference. At 262144 the CUDA compute
# buffer is 11 136 MiB at -ub 8192 -- not the 7040 the 131072 profile sees, because
# the buffer scales with CONTEXT as well as with -ub. That leaves 7176 MiB spare at
# n21; n20 has 3912 and dies at depth, n19 does not load at all.
: "${IK_NCMOE:=21}"

# 0: the experts that live in host RAM are computed ON THE GPU, not on the CPU.
# See RESULTS §21 for the mechanism and §27 for this profile's conversion.
: "${IK_RTR:=0}"
: "${IK_UBATCH:=8192}"
: "${IK_BATCH:=8192}"

# MLA latent cache: f16, never q8_0 (§10.2).
: "${IK_CTK:=f16}"
: "${IK_CTV:=f16}"

# --cache-ram 32768 is a memory guard, not a tuning choice ---------------------
# Checkpoints are worth keeping here -- tools/ckpt-value.sh on real Hermes logs
# priced them at 8.4 : 1 in their favour (372 creations cost 118 s, 17 restores
# saved 1106) -- but they need a ceiling, because at 262144 one checkpoint is
# 1743 MiB and the default --ctx-checkpoints 32 authorises 54.5 GiB inside a
# default byte budget of 8192 MiB that upstream does not enforce.
#
# We do enforce it: the local patch in docs/external/local-cache-limit.patch trims
# checkpoints oldest-first once the limit is exceeded (filed as a comment on
# ikawrakow/ik_llama.cpp#2320). 32 GiB keeps roughly 18 checkpoints, which is far
# more than the 4.6 % that ever get used.
#
# This is not hypothetical. On 2026-08-19 a sweep arm ran the 524288 profile with
# -ctx-ckpt 32 and no ceiling; the server reached 231 GiB RSS and the OOM killer
# took it and the user's editor with it. The arithmetic said 211 of 244 GiB "fits"
# -- it fits the model, not the desktop.
: "${IK_EXTRA_ARGS:=-mla 3 -fidx --reasoning-format deepseek -nkvo --cache-ram 32768}"
